EUGENE GIRARD: Awesome.

00:04:44.248 --> 00:04:46.534
Thanks, Chad.

00:04:46.534 --> 00:04:48.325
So obviously, we're
talking about BigQuery,

00:04:48.325 --> 00:04:50.710
so we're going to be
talking about SQL here.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:52.440
And the important
thing about SQL

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.771
is that you've got to
know your data if you're

00:04:54.771 --> 00:04:55.770
going to make use of it.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.922
Little-- thank you.

00:04:57.922 --> 00:05:01.294
So when I started learning
SQL, way back in university,

00:05:01.294 --> 00:05:03.710
I ran a lot of queries that
kind of looked like this where

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:07.270
you select some stuff from
a bunch of rows and columns,

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.202
and you do a select
statement that

00:05:09.202 --> 00:05:11.160
said which column, which
rows you were actually

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.220
interested in.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.080
And then you add it
up with something

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.830
down at the bottom
that kind of showed you

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:15.960
what you're looking at.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.570
The thing there is
that it was always

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.790
kind of a very grid-like look.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.130
You have all these rows
and columns of data,

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:24.990
and you kind of
know exactly what

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.340
you're going to get out of it.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:32.760
Now that's got me through CS
322 just fine a few years ago.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.400
The world's changed, and we've
got a slightly more robust

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.440
and capable engine in BigQuery
that can represent a lot more

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.450
than just rows and columns.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.660
So a lot of these things are
things like key value pairs.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.730
There are things like
records or objects.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:50.250
There's things like arrays
of objects or records

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.050
or key value pairs
or all those things.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.540
And all of that gets
a little involved.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.514
So you have to really
understand your schema

00:05:56.514 --> 00:05:57.930
if you're going
to be able to make

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:02.700
use of a big table like this.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:07.490
So this is the schema that we
generate as part of analytics

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:08.240
from Firebase.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.649
And the thing here, we're going
to go through a few of these.

00:06:11.649 --> 00:06:13.940
A lot of these things we
really don't care about today.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.106
And you can kind of ignore
the parts you don't need,

00:06:16.106 --> 00:06:17.910
that you're not
using at the moment.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.505
But we're going to focus
on a couple of these.

00:06:20.505 --> 00:06:22.880
So right off the bat, there's
a couple of key value pairs

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:23.980
that are really important.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:27.470
There's things like the date
and the timestamp, and also

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.000
the event name.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.190
Every event that the developer
emits in their Firebase app

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:37.260
gets its own unique
kind of schema here.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.410
And we always key
off the event name

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:42.700
to say what kind of an
event are we looking at.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:44.700
Afterwards, there's a
bunch of event parameters.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:45.900
This is an array.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:48.430
You can throw in a many
things you want here.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:52.697
And in those, there's
a name for the key.

00:06:52.697 --> 00:06:54.280
So that says-- for
example, here we're

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.630
looking at the final score
when your level is done.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:01.260
And that's going to have an
int value associated with it.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:04.230
Just to make these easier
to use we throw in, like,

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.340
four different values.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.620
Only one of those is ever
going to be non-null.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.140
Every single final score
will always be using the end,

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.120
and all the others
are going to be null.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.340
And something like
a level name we'd

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.470
be using the string value and
all the others would be null.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.220
So this makes it very
predictable from event

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.390
to event.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.850
But the actual values
that are filled in you're

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.090
going to have to kind of know
your schema for your own events

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:30.030
that you're generating
in your app.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:33.425
And this is where your own app
can specialize and generate

00:07:33.425 --> 00:07:35.050
the kind of events
that you care about.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.940
So we also have a user ID.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:43.350
This is something that the
app can set if they choose to.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:46.960
And we also generated a
pseudo ID for the user.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.180
The pseudo ID is unique
to that user for one

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.710
run of the app on one device.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.940
So this is what we use to tie
in a bunch of events together

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.370
to say that they're from
the same run of that app.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.160
And this is the one that
we're keying in all the time.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.850
The user ID, again,
it's optional

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.190
if you use that or not.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:05.740
In our sample data we've
actually nulled all of those

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:10.430
out, because we don't want any
PI leaking out in our demos.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.560
So we have a slightly
sanitized, pseudonymized version

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.240
of the data that we're
going to be working with.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.890
There's also a bunch
of user properties

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:23.270
that get associated with
that row in the database.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:26.210
And the nice thing about
throwing the user properties

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.880
here is that those
properties change

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.190
a lot over the run of an app.

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:33.500
And this way you can
see what the properties

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.200
were at the moment that
that particular event fired.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.450
And that makes it
really easy to have

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:41.450
complex things like the
experience level, which

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.070
for this user is
going to change.

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:44.690
Basically every time
there's an event,

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:47.122
their XP is going to be slightly
different at that point.

00:08:47.122 --> 00:08:48.830
So it lets you see at
that moment in time

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:50.750
what the values were.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.650
We also have whether they're
a premium player or not.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:56.720
All of this data is up
to the app developer

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:58.580
to decide what they
want to stow into this.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:04.407
We also track a bunch
of useful metrics.

00:09:04.407 --> 00:09:06.240
Firebase supports a lot
of different devices

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:07.614
and a lot of
different platforms.

00:09:07.614 --> 00:09:10.080
So it will let you track
down where the users are,

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.520
what devices they're using,
where they came from,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.776
which exact version of
your app they're using.

00:09:16.776 --> 00:09:18.150
If you're doing
A/B testing, that

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.370
would also show up
there, the platform

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:21.510
that you're running on.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:25.390
All of this kind that
goes into the app itself.

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:27.060
And so every app
that you write is

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:29.250
going to have its
own unique concerns

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.350
about what the user's up
to or what it's doing.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.150
And these are the kind of
things that you're going

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.441
to want to ask questions about.

00:09:34.441 --> 00:09:37.184
So here's an example of
the Bingo Blast game.

00:09:37.184 --> 00:09:38.850
It's a game that's
out in the app store.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:39.974
You can run it and play it.

00:09:39.974 --> 00:09:41.660
It's actually kind of fun.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:45.060
But we use that to demonstrate
some of the capabilities

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:45.690
of Firebase.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:48.250
It happens to spit out
a couple of events.

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:52.320
So every time you finish
around in Bingo Blast

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.660
we create a round
completed event.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.000
And we tie-in a bunch of
things like the score.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.640
Whether the game was a
solo or a social game--

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:01.370
the type of game
that you're playing.

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:04.862
We also keep track the user
data down at the bottom,

00:10:04.862 --> 00:10:07.320
whether or not this user happens
to be signed in right now.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.611
And if they are, we want to
know whether they have ever

00:10:09.611 --> 00:10:10.640
spent money on the game.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.181
Because at the end
of the day, that's

00:10:12.181 --> 00:10:14.260
a very interesting
metric to be looking at.

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:16.824
We also keep track of things
like XP, which is really

00:10:16.824 --> 00:10:18.990
a measure of how long they've
been playing the game,

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:22.300
and how experienced
you'd expect them to be.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:24.240
So with all of that
background, I'm

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:25.860
going to turn it
back over to Chad

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.380
and ask him to walk you through
how to use this kind of data.

