00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:00.540
I guess it will--

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.024
further out here we've
got the user events.

00:14:03.024 --> 00:14:04.440
So that's still--
like, the result

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.360
here is still a nested
and repeated field.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:11.070
So what we want to
do is let's just

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:12.720
pick out all of the event--

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:15.740
or the event params where the
key is equal to score, right?

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:16.820
We want to find out--

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.530
like, the goal of this question
is, like, what are the scores?

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:22.950
And let's do some stats on the
scores that people are playing.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:26.320
And as you can tell, right,
we're getting an error here.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:28.260
So why are we getting
an error here, right?

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:32.880
One of the event
parameters is score.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.920
But the problem is event
params is an array.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:38.225
So you can't-- you have to
do something to event params

00:14:38.225 --> 00:14:38.725
first.

00:14:38.725 --> 00:14:39.120
All right.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.120
So let's go ahead and
switch back to the slides.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.830
And we'll walk through what you
have to do with event params

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:44.402
to get there.

00:14:44.402 --> 00:14:46.110
Can we switch back to
the slides, please?

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:51.540
All righty.

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:53.020
So here's event params.

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:56.190
And here's the problem,
there's no .key property.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:57.370
It's part of this.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.640
So we have to
extract that first.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:02.820
And so unnest to the rescue.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:04.660
So for those of you
who are SQL-minded,

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:07.080
unnest acts like a cross join.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.900
For those of you who
are table-minded,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.690
unnest turns nested and
repeated into a table.

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:15.820
So let's see how that works.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:19.710
So in this example, we want
to find all the starships

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.052
where crew member equals Zoe.

00:15:21.052 --> 00:15:23.010
So if you do it like
this, this is what we just

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:24.742
did in the BigQuery UI.

00:15:24.742 --> 00:15:26.950
If you want to do this,
you're going to get an error.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:28.860
So you've got to
unnest it first.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:30.580
So select star from starships.

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.850
That was what we did in
the very first query.

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:35.730
And if you cross join--

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:40.157
if you wrote this out, right,
this would actually work.

00:15:40.157 --> 00:15:42.240
You know, so this will
actually produce this table

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:44.040
where you blow out--
you create a new row

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:47.790
for every member of the
event params with crew names.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:50.700
And then you can filter on
that by picking the crew member

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:51.540
that you want.

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:54.750
But they're simpler syntax
to do that where you can just

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.280
use this unnest command.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:59.020
Again, all of this code
is in the GitHub repo.

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:01.560
So feel free to take pictures,
but you have access to it

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:03.270
at the end.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.540
And now you can filter on the
parameter you want, right?

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:10.050
So instead of just doing this
filter on crew member as that

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:12.020
first-- like, in that
first letter query--

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.910
unnest first, and then you can
filter on what you want, right?

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:17.970
Turn the array into
a table with unnest,

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.740
and then you filter
the way you like.

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:21.520
There we go.

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.770
And who would have thought
that Zoe likes wingspans

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:28.630
of 32.4 whatever the units are.

00:16:28.630 --> 00:16:32.650
OK, so in the context
of our example here

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:34.150
we want to do the
same thing, right?

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:35.750
We want to pick the score param.

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:39.220
So unnest event params,
and then filter--

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:40.560
or do the where--

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.190
on score, right?

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:46.360
And that filters out
the row that we want.

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:50.820
OK, oh, and then here
one thing I should say.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:54.820
If you select star here, you're
going to get the event params

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:56.580
array in your answer.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:58.950
And that can just make your
answer a little bit messy,

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:00.630
like a little larger
than you need.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:03.510
So there's a neat tip
here where you select star

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:06.560
except for the event params,
and you just cut that out.

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:07.200
Up to you.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.270
I thought that was a cool trick.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:09.900
All right.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:12.630
Let's go back to
the demo, and we'll

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:15.339
get to where we want to go.

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:20.880
So here's that same
command where we unnest.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.530
And then you can see what's
happened here to the results,

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:24.030
right?

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:26.339
The results now
look like a table.

00:17:26.339 --> 00:17:27.270
And then we can look--

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:28.770
what we're going
to do next is we'll

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.260
look through the
param.key key and find

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:34.580
all of the score events.

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:35.960
All right, so here it is.

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:39.290
So unnesting where event
name equals round completed

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.690
and where the param
key equals score.

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:43.100
And there we have it.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:45.050
OK, so that's pretty cool.

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:48.470
So that's how you deal with
the top level of schema.

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:50.600
But remember, the goal was
we want to do something

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:53.670
with these numbers.

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:55.257
So we'll go ahead and select.

00:17:55.257 --> 00:17:56.840
Instead of just the
event param, we're

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:59.720
going to select the-- like,
instead of select star,

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.360
we'll pull out the stuff
that we actually want.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:06.620
And as Eugene showed
you, right, we'll

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:08.600
just pull out the
int value for score,

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:11.450
since that's the one that's
not null for this parameter.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:12.170
OK, cool.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.960
And then we can actually
do some arithmetic on it

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:20.890
right in the select.

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:22.650
So here we have--
so we're calculating

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:24.730
the average of the int value.

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:26.740
And we're calculating
the standard deviation,

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:28.080
and then returning those.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:32.027
So, I mean, as
analysts on a game,

00:18:32.027 --> 00:18:34.110
you can then run this back
to your game developers

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:37.170
and say you know, hey, the
standard deviation is way

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:38.369
too high.

00:18:38.369 --> 00:18:40.410
I don't know, maybe that
translates to a bad user

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:41.430
experience in some way.

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:42.888
But this is data
that you can start

00:18:42.888 --> 00:18:47.340
to use to see whatever
you want to see.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.180
In any can, let us now--

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:51.720
let's see what the
message here is.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.595
We're going to go ahead
and start number two.

00:18:53.595 --> 00:18:57.130
Let's go ahead and switch
back to the slides, please.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:18:57.700
Cool.

00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:58.660
All right.

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:01.550
So finished number one.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:03.880
On to number two with,
how does this score

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:06.040
correlate with the
user's experience level?

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:08.010
OK, back to the demo, please.

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:12.560
Okey-doke.

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:16.320
So starting with the same
query that we had before--

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:18.390
right, this is
exactly the same one--

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:20.710
we want to correlate this
with user's experience.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:22.440
So we're going to
have-- so remember,

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:24.459
the event parameters
and the user experiences

00:19:24.459 --> 00:19:27.000
were in two different-- remember
from what Eugene was talking

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.940
about-- those are in
two different nests

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:31.420
inside a single row.

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:35.430
So we're going to have to unnest
that second parameter here--

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:39.450
or sorry-- that second
array I should say.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:43.800
Well, boy-- like, not
clairvoyance, right.

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:45.310
I just managed to
get that right.

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:46.060
And so here we go.

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:48.580
We're going to unnest
user properties.

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:51.870
And then you can see
that that blows out,

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.150
or it just increases
the scope of the table.

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:56.460
So the answers include the
user properties as well as

00:19:56.460 --> 00:20:01.536
the event properties,
again, filtered by a score.

00:20:01.536 --> 00:20:04.590
So I'm saving myself the trouble
of running every query here

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:06.190
live.

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:09.260
But now how do we
correlate between the two?

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:09.880
All right.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:14.820
So next step is go ahead and
filter by the user property

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:15.440
that you want.

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:18.325
So we wanted to correlate
score with experience points.

00:20:18.325 --> 00:20:19.950
So we'll go ahead
and filter those out.

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:20.920
So we get the table.

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:23.350
So for round completed
events, here's the score.

00:20:23.350 --> 00:20:27.750
Here's the user
experience points.

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:31.320
And, yeah, this step--

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:34.110
oh, yeah, we should
point out almost all

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:38.880
of the user parameters
come back as strings.

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:41.310
So before we actually do
some computation on it

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:44.340
we have to cast the
string as an int,

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.800
and call that experience points.

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:50.880
And here is the beginning
of the next one.

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:52.980
Sorry, here's the beginning
of the next trick.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.105
We'll go through this one
in a little bit of detail

00:20:55.105 --> 00:20:57.190
in just a second, but
I'll introduce it here.

00:20:57.190 --> 00:21:02.720
So in that last query, right,
we selected event name, and then

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:04.920
the params as the score.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:08.270
Then we cast the
experience points into it.

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:10.380
So we've got all the
data that we want here,

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.780
but now you have to
calculate the correlation

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:13.930
between the two.

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:16.140
And for those of
you who aren't SQL,

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:19.200
or who weren't born
speaking SQL like me,

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.800
a very clever way to do
that-- and for those--

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.590
the other problem with
this construct in SQL

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.180
is it's kind of
counter-intuitive

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:29.640
if you've learned another
programming language first.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.950
And so what this is
called is a sub select.

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:34.200
So what I've highlighted
here is the query

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:36.750
that generated the
table that we wanted.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:39.660
And then you can take
that inside a SQL query

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:41.140
and write a query
on top of that,

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:42.630
which is this top row here.

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:46.750
And then do operations on
the result of the subquery.

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:47.980
So that's kind of cool.

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:50.280
We'll go through that in
a little bit of detail.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:53.610
But here's the correlation
between experience points

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:54.700
and score.

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:56.820
So you can see that folks
with a lot of experience

00:21:56.820 --> 00:22:01.010
have a slightly positive
correlation to better scores.

00:22:01.010 --> 00:22:02.876
I don't know, bingo
is mostly random.

00:22:02.876 --> 00:22:05.250
But if you get the hang of
it, you're better than random.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:07.510
I guess that's what that says.

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:11.650
So for those of you who
gamble on bingo, beware.

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.200
That was a joke everyone.

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:15.160
Thank you.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:15.700
All right.

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:19.144
Let's go back to the demo.

00:22:19.144 --> 00:22:21.060
I'll just queue up the
next query-- oh, sorry,

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:22.380
go back to the slides.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:26.260
And we'll go through
what the sub selects are.

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:30.430
So Yeah, correlation of
score with experience.

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:31.894
Subqueries make life easier.

00:22:31.894 --> 00:22:33.060
So here we'll go through it.

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:37.080
So we have this
schema for Google--

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:40.110
yeah, the schema for Firebase
Analytics inside of BigQuery.

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.210
And then you do some
operation on it, right?

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.880
That was that query where
we pulled out the parameters

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.780
that we wanted, where
we did the unnesting

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:50.530
to turn it into a table, and
we get the table that we want.

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:52.410
Now we want to do an
operation on this.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:55.530
In the example, this was
doing the correlation

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:58.210
between experience
points and score.

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:01.590
So we want to turn that
into this other table.

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:03.180
And we do that with
a query that goes

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:05.560
around the outside
of that first one.

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:09.120
So here, I'll just
get to this story.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:10.110
So here's a long one.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.270
We're not going to go
through this one in detail.

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:17.190
But the way you read queries
in SQL is not the way you read

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:18.810
Python or-- like in my case--

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:21.270
Matlab or C or C sharp, right?

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:23.520
Where you define stuff at
the top of the file and then

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:24.930
you kind of go down.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.660
In SQL you start here
kind of at the bottom.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:30.090
Like, you read from
the middle out, right?

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.800
So that's the base query
that's going into the table--

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:35.580
in our case, like, pulling
out and unnesting stuff.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:39.420
And then you operate on that
with a query that's above it.

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:40.980
And then, you know,
your final query

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:44.090
you're doing a little bit of
counting and then grouping by.

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:47.850
But, like, realizing that
this is how SQL worked

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:49.920
was a total watershed
moment for me.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.770
And so hopefully that's helpful
for the folks who are, like,

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:56.310
deeply steeped in writing
games but not so steeped

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:57.840
in writing SQL.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:00.210
Very, very helpful trick.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:02.100
All righty.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:04.780
So we'll go back to the
code to finish number two.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:07.740
Actually we already
finished number two.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.962
Let's go back to the
slides and we'll move on.

00:24:10.962 --> 00:24:11.810
All right.

00:24:11.810 --> 00:24:13.720
So we covered the correlation.

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:17.240
Next one is how do we break
out score by game type?

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:17.740
All right.

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:19.300
So switching back to the demo.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:24.470
So you would think this one
would be super easy, right?

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:26.040
We just figured out this unnest.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:29.850
Unnest works
beautifully when you've

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:37.440
got two different
nests in the same row.

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:39.180
But this problem is--

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:41.910
sorry, this question is actually
going to run us into a problem

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:44.540
where game type is actually
stored in a different row.

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:47.550
So we'll have to see how
we deal with that one.

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:50.590
So here is the way to do it.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:53.490
So in a subquery,
in this line, we're

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:55.740
going to go into our
event params table

00:24:55.740 --> 00:25:00.000
and pull out all
of the event params

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.125
where the key equals score.

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:13.300
And so that's kind of what we
did in the previous example.

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.130
And now since we
need game type we

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:17.710
have to go-- we
have to unnest again

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:19.390
and pull out the game type.

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:22.570
So you'll see this come up,
especially in Felipe's section.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:27.770
And we'll go through this
in a second in the demo.

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:30.820
But when you-- so here
we pull out event name.

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:31.660
We unnest.

00:25:31.660 --> 00:25:34.450
So we've now turned
these two nested fields

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:35.940
into a really big table.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:40.820
And then we can also--

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:42.140
we can clean this up again.

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:45.230
Give these some names to
make the SQL a lot readable.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:47.690
By the way, these aliases--

00:25:47.690 --> 00:25:52.850
super useful to make
your SQL readable.

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:53.930
It's fun.

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:55.880
As a product manager
working on BigQuery,

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:58.580
I work with people who are
literally born speaking SQL.

00:25:58.580 --> 00:26:00.830
And so they don't do stuff
like this, because this all

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:01.710
makes sense to them.

00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:03.110
And then they send
me a query like, hey,

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:04.651
Chad what do you
think of this query?

00:26:04.651 --> 00:26:06.170
I'm like, I can't tell.

00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:08.630
So if you want your
code readable, do that.

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:09.530
That's a good thing.

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:13.952
And so here we're
using the sub select.

00:26:13.952 --> 00:26:15.410
So in this, how
many lines of code?

00:26:15.410 --> 00:26:18.590
One, two, three, four, five,
six, seven lines of code.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:22.520
We're doing four-- sorry--
three subselects and one select.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:24.980
So you can tell this
trick of using subselects

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.020
is really, really prevalent.

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:30.200
So select from unnest
to get the score.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:32.570
Select from unnest--
whoops, sorry--

00:26:32.570 --> 00:26:35.960
select from unnest to
get the type of game.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:40.790
And that is all executed
inside this subquery.

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:43.130
And then we're filtering
to get the round completed.

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:47.270
And then in the outermost
query, we're doing the math

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:49.430
and getting the average
score for the game type.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:52.520
And so here-- down here, like,
here's the result, right?

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:55.520
So we see-- and again, this
is the thing you take back

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:56.750
to the developer team.

00:26:56.750 --> 00:26:58.190
If you're on the
analyst team you

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:01.700
say, look, folks are doing
much better in the social game

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:03.800
than they are in the solo game.

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:04.729
Is that what we want?

00:27:04.729 --> 00:27:06.770
Is that the good thing,
or is that the bad thing?

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:09.980
But having this detail,
you know, it's good.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:12.470
Especially if that causes
you to make a change

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:15.050
or to double down on a
behavior that you like.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:15.550
All right.

00:27:15.550 --> 00:27:16.790
Make sense?

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:17.300
Cool.

00:27:17.300 --> 00:27:19.340
Again, all the
code is in GitHub.

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:22.610
So you can sit with that
as much as you like.

00:27:22.610 --> 00:27:23.130
All right.

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:26.510
We'll go ahead and switch
back to the slides, please.

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:34.090
Let me just get this window
set up for the next one.

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:34.590
All right.

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:36.450
Cool.

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:38.580
So select from unnest.

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:42.180
Let's go through a little
bit of an example of how

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:44.950
this operation really works.

00:27:44.950 --> 00:27:45.930
So here we are.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:49.530
So selecting event name
from the events table,

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:50.670
from this big table.

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:52.680
And you see the gold
bricks here represent

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:55.570
the different key
values in the array.

00:27:55.570 --> 00:28:00.930
So we'll go ahead and unnest
event params and search

00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:02.760
on score.

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:04.940
Come along.

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:05.460
There we go.

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:05.960
All right.

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:07.430
So we unnest the key.

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:10.740
And then we're going to
filter that and just pick out

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:14.490
where key equals score.

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:15.880
There we go.

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:20.480
And that will put in a score
column and expand our table.

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:23.320
So, again, turning the
nested array into a table.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:26.650
And then we're going
to do that again.

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:27.220
All right.

00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:29.178
Now we're going to go in
and get type of games.

00:28:29.178 --> 00:28:31.420
So still going into
the event params,

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:35.920
and this one we're going
to pull out type of game.

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:37.810
And that adds a
column over here.

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:40.930
And now this is kind of
like a regular table.

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:44.050
I find this one much easier to
hold in my head conceptually.

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:46.180
And then applying,
you know, filters

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:48.280
where event name
equals round completed

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:50.230
or where the game
type equals whatever,

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:52.270
like, that kind of
operation in SQL

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:53.590
is far more intuitive to me.

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:55.048
So once you get
through this part--

00:28:55.048 --> 00:28:57.760
like once you get everything
into this kind of tabular

00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:58.570
format--

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:02.650
then operating on it, I
think, gets a lot easier.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:03.150
All right.

00:29:03.150 --> 00:29:04.500
We'll go back to the demo.

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:08.580
Actually, to be honest,
I think we've finished

00:29:08.580 --> 00:29:10.200
this part of the demo already.

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:14.200
So why don't we stay
on the slides, please.

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:15.280
Yap.

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:18.700
So we finished breaking
out the score by game type.

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:21.360
We're going to skip over four.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:22.750
Felipe is going to do five.

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:26.565
So now we're going to literally
cut right to the money,

00:29:26.565 --> 00:29:27.940
and we're going
to do an analysis

00:29:27.940 --> 00:29:31.900
to figure out what are the
patterns that end with spending

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:35.740
virtual currency, right, since
this is what makes the world go

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:39.340
around in some circles.

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:40.730
Okey-doke.

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:41.430
Now we go back.

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:43.940
Thanks.

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:44.930
All right.

00:29:44.930 --> 00:29:46.670
Here, let me go ahead
and maximize this

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:50.770
so that everybody
can see a bit easier.

00:29:50.770 --> 00:29:53.670
OK, so again with
the select star.

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:56.270
Just to show you
what the report--

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:57.840
sorry-- what the
result looks like.

00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:00.000
And we're looking
for event names

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.970
where the event is
spend virtual currency.

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:03.750
Here we go.

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:05.200
That filter works.

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:07.320
And then, like, as
you see out here,

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:10.360
like, event params has
a bunch in the array.

00:30:10.360 --> 00:30:16.450
So let's start
working with that.

00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:20.760
So even if you want to just
take a quick look, you can--

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:24.520
pulling out event names,
user pseudo ID and timestamp.

00:30:24.520 --> 00:30:27.610
And then ordering by
pseudo ID and timestamp,

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:32.500
you can start to get a look
at what the things were

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:35.650
that a particular pseudo--
or a user pseudo ID

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:37.360
went ahead and did, right?

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:39.340
Because we're
ordering by timestamp,

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:42.850
right, they initialized the
API, session start, logging in.

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:45.630
The problem with this is that--

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:47.040
I can't quite show--

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:49.380
is that you can actually
get some mixed results

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:51.340
when people--

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:53.720
or different people-- are
interacting with the game.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:55.670
So you've got to be a
little bit clever here.

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:56.170
All right.

00:30:56.170 --> 00:30:57.520
So let's go back to the slides.

00:30:57.520 --> 00:31:00.720
We're going to introduce
another topic calling lead--

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:03.472
are called Using
LEAD to Peek Ahead.

00:31:03.472 --> 00:31:04.930
So this is a trick
that we're going

00:31:04.930 --> 00:31:07.310
to employ in the demo in
just a couple of minutes.

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:09.430
So what we want
to do here, right,

00:31:09.430 --> 00:31:11.590
because we're trying to
identify-- like in the demo

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:13.970
we're going to identify
these strings of events.

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:16.780
So the user did this, then the
user did that, user did this.

00:31:16.780 --> 00:31:19.660
And this is paramount to, like,
looking ahead a little bit

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:20.710
in the database.

00:31:20.710 --> 00:31:23.590
So with our friends--

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:26.800
actually with the exception of
Mortimer, this kind of looks

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:29.830
like names from a pop band.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:30.780
I don't know Mortimer.

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:32.880
Maybe Mortimer could be
a good pop band name.

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:36.100
Any case, we're going to take
we're going to take this,

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:39.310
and we're going to figure out
how we look at the next age.

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:44.720
So the command to do
that is lead by name.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:49.150
So we're going to look ahead
by one space over order by age.

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:51.340
So we're going to
order this table by age

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:53.210
and then look ahead one space.

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:56.950
So in memory, BigQuery
creates this other table

00:31:56.950 --> 00:31:58.580
where it's ordered by--

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:01.870
there we go-- where
it's ordered by age.

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:05.110
And then you can see that
as we look at Mortimer,

00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:08.200
the next oldest person
in this list is Summer.

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:10.750
So that's going to be the
answer to our question of,

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:15.550
like, what's the next one
looking ahead in the Database

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:16.860
And then we assign that.

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:21.106
We can create a whole new
column here as next oldest.

00:32:21.106 --> 00:32:22.480
So let's go back
and see how that

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:31.200
fits into our construct of doing
this work in our example here.

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:33.710
So-- whoops-- so here we go.

00:32:33.710 --> 00:32:35.320
So we have lead over--

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:39.700
so we're going to order by
pseudo ID and event timestamp.

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:42.070
And so you can see here that--

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:46.990
so when the event name is,
like, initialize the API,

00:32:46.990 --> 00:32:51.220
looking ahead-- like, looking
at the next event for that user

00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:52.780
ordered by timestamp--

00:32:52.780 --> 00:32:54.936
you can see that the next
event is session start.

00:32:54.936 --> 00:32:56.560
So you can actually
start to get a look

00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:59.750
at a funnel for this
particular user ID, right?

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:02.070
But that's not-- like, we
don't want to look at what,

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:05.410
you know, Bob, Jane,
Sally, Phil, whatever,

00:33:05.410 --> 00:33:06.830
what that person did.

00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:09.880
We want to look in aggregate
and see what our users are doing

00:33:09.880 --> 00:33:13.270
and which one is the most
popular funnel, or most

00:33:13.270 --> 00:33:14.830
popular path through
our game that

00:33:14.830 --> 00:33:16.600
ends in spend virtual
currency, right?

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:17.590
Make sense?

00:33:17.590 --> 00:33:18.221
All right.

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:24.990
Here's another
trick, by the way.

00:33:24.990 --> 00:33:27.410
Including this
partition by command

00:33:27.410 --> 00:33:31.430
guarantees that the segments
of the table you're looking at

00:33:31.430 --> 00:33:34.430
are partitioned on
the user's pseudo ID.

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:35.840
If you don't put
that, you're not

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:40.220
guaranteed to get all the
block of user that ends in BA5.

00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:43.240
You're not guaranteed to get all
of them together in one chunk.

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:43.740
All right.

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:45.170
A very useful tip.

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:47.990
I'm sure that's going to save
somebody, like, one all-nighter

00:33:47.990 --> 00:33:49.919
of heartache right there.

00:33:49.919 --> 00:33:51.710
That's our good deed
for the day, everyone.

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:54.120
Well done, well done.

00:33:54.120 --> 00:33:55.640
Okey-doke.

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:57.800
So we're going to peek ahead.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:33:59.490
Oh, yeah, and sorry--

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:03.030
a funnel is not super useful
if it just has one step in it.

00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:05.600
So we're going to look
at the event name, right?

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:07.850
Eventually we're going to
look at that event name that

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:09.860
ends in spend virtual currency.

00:34:09.860 --> 00:34:13.730
And we'll look ahead one
spot, and we'll call that one,

00:34:13.730 --> 00:34:15.006
you know, S1.

00:34:15.006 --> 00:34:16.714
And then we'll look
ahead one other spot,

00:34:16.714 --> 00:34:18.400
and we'll call that one S2.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:21.144
So we'll get this nice little
triplet of they did this,

00:34:21.144 --> 00:34:23.060
they did this, and then,
boom, that ended with

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:26.300
spend virtual currency.

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:26.949
So here we are.

00:34:26.949 --> 00:34:29.032
Here's where we introduced
the filter that defines

00:34:29.032 --> 00:34:31.120
the endpoint for our funnel.

00:34:31.120 --> 00:34:33.830
And that starts to generate
these cool little triplets.

00:34:33.830 --> 00:34:34.750
They log in.

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:36.420
They spun some slots.

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:38.030
Spend virtual currency.

00:34:38.030 --> 00:34:38.980
So that's cool, right?

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:40.480
Now we know a whole
bunch of tricks.

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:44.080
But the question now becomes,
you know, great Chad.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:46.320
We now have like
how many results?

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:49.449
Like we now have 1,000--
here, down here at the bottom.

00:34:49.449 --> 00:34:53.020
We now have 1,057 different
things that ended up

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:55.480
with spend virtual currency.

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:57.220
Which one is the most important?

00:34:57.220 --> 00:34:58.720
Well, fortunately
with the tricks

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:03.260
that we learned before, we
can figure that one out.

00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:06.080
Here let me expand
this a little bit.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:07.550
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:35:07.550 --> 00:35:10.526
going to take that same
query that we had before,

00:35:10.526 --> 00:35:11.650
and we're going to wrap it.

00:35:11.650 --> 00:35:12.730
We're going to
make it a subquery.

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:15.220
And we're going to wrap it in
a larger one where we select

00:35:15.220 --> 00:35:17.300
the elements that we want.

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:18.850
We count the ones.

00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:22.120
And then this code down here
goes with the outer query,

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:24.020
right, because it's outside.

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:27.260
See, because it's outside
of the parentheses.

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:28.930
We'll order by count descending.

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:29.660
So here we go.

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:33.700
So here is the most popular or
the most often used trajectory

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:36.160
that ends in spin
virtual currently,

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:39.070
goes with select content,
and round completed.

00:35:39.070 --> 00:35:40.912
Round completed,
that's interesting.

00:35:40.912 --> 00:35:42.370
When they complete
a round, they're

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:44.890
more apt to spend
virtual currency.

00:35:44.890 --> 00:35:47.680
There is a bit of interesting
information that--

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:49.660
you know, now you can
create these funnels

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:52.249
and figure out where are the
places that we need to invest.

00:35:52.249 --> 00:35:54.790
Or maybe you look at this and
you're like, oh, you know what?

00:35:54.790 --> 00:35:56.800
I really shop page viewed.

00:35:56.800 --> 00:35:58.750
I'd really rather
this be the funnel

00:35:58.750 --> 00:36:00.470
that drives the most value.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:02.470
What do we need to do to
our design to push that

00:36:02.470 --> 00:36:04.850
one higher up on the list?

00:36:04.850 --> 00:36:09.660
OK, let's go back to
the slides real quick.

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:10.970
So we did that.

00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:12.850
Everybody gets a
checkmark for number six.

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:13.940
Well done, everyone.

00:36:13.940 --> 00:36:15.950
You've accomplished
something today.

00:36:15.950 --> 00:36:18.020
And now we're going
to kick it right over

00:36:18.020 --> 00:36:19.610
to Felipe, who's
going to show you

00:36:19.610 --> 00:36:21.964
how to take one of these
queries and customize

00:36:21.964 --> 00:36:22.880
the dashboard with it.

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:25.846
So over to you, sir.

00:36:25.846 --> 00:36:27.096
FELIPE HOFFA: Thank you, Chad.

00:36:27.096 --> 00:36:29.270
Hey, I'm loud now.

00:36:29.270 --> 00:36:31.550
Thank you, Chad.

00:36:31.550 --> 00:36:33.200
How are you doing so far?

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:34.030
Good.

00:36:34.030 --> 00:36:35.200
Yes, I love Chad.

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:36.680
I love Eugene.

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:39.020
I try to be quick,
because I love speaking.

00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:40.840
I'm a developer advocate.

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:43.190
But today you have the
chance to ask questions

00:36:43.190 --> 00:36:46.100
to my favorite product
managers, so please

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:49.220
get your questions ready.

00:36:49.220 --> 00:36:52.670
Who is familiar
with Data Studio?

00:36:52.670 --> 00:36:53.222
Good.

00:36:53.222 --> 00:36:53.930
Good, good, good.

00:36:53.930 --> 00:36:57.210
Are you using Data Studio
with Firebase already?

00:36:57.210 --> 00:36:59.010
OK, let me show you.

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:01.070
So we've been writing
a lot of queries.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:03.890
Just for the ones that
don't know data Studio,

00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:05.990
I want to connect
it to BigQuery,

00:37:05.990 --> 00:37:09.110
and show you how to extend
the two with the queries we've

00:37:09.110 --> 00:37:10.930
been running.

00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:17.450
So if you look for the Firebase
Data Studio report template,

00:37:17.450 --> 00:37:19.410
you will find this page.

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:23.490
And this will point you
straight to the template.

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:25.530
And the template is pretty nice.

00:37:25.530 --> 00:37:28.820
It's connected to
a sample data set.

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:29.810
You can play with it.

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:31.470
You can run your filters.

00:37:31.470 --> 00:37:32.630
You can--

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:36.080
Someone already figured out
interesting visualizations

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:38.810
to do with existing data--

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:41.810
dashboards, advanced,
conversions.

00:37:41.810 --> 00:37:44.630
Now, this doesn't have my data.

00:37:44.630 --> 00:37:48.670
So if I want to pull-- connect
it to my data set in the query,

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:51.900
I just need to
click use template.

00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:54.820
And instead of connecting it
to an existing data source,

00:37:54.820 --> 00:37:59.710
I can create a new data source
where I choose BigQuery.

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:01.840
I choose my billing project.

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:05.380
I choose mine, where my data is.

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:10.240
And then I get the template
that I can edit and change

00:38:10.240 --> 00:38:12.160
in any way that I want.

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:13.480
And I'm getting visual results.

00:38:13.480 --> 00:38:15.280
I can share this with my team.

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:18.070
I can let my team filter
by whatever they want.

00:38:18.070 --> 00:38:20.190
Let's get all the
Android results.

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:20.830
Perfect.

00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:25.310
This goes on the background to
BigQuery [INAUDIBLE] things.

00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:28.130
So let's look at events.

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:31.540
And here I can search
for an event name.

00:38:31.540 --> 00:38:34.190
There's post score, for example.

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:36.450
And I can only look
at those events

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:38.690
and whatever name I want.

00:38:38.690 --> 00:38:43.100
Where the score, for
example, is wherever I want.

00:38:43.100 --> 00:38:44.250
And this is pretty cool.

00:38:44.250 --> 00:38:48.110
Now I don't need to write
SQL queries anymore.

00:38:48.110 --> 00:38:51.580
Except that there are many
things that I cannot solve just

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:54.170
with an interactive report.

00:38:54.170 --> 00:38:55.880
Sometimes I want
to write smarter

00:38:55.880 --> 00:38:58.840
queries, more complex queries.

00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:02.080
And we can get back
to BigQuery for that.

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:05.230
So if I get back
to BigQuery, one

00:39:05.230 --> 00:39:06.790
of the cool things
you will notice

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:09.650
is as Data Studio
is running queries

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:12.880
on BigQuery on your
behalf, you get

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:15.100
to see all the queries that
Data Studio is running.

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:19.120
So I can open the
queries here, and I can--

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:21.580
let me format this query.

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:24.140
You can see the queries
that Data Studio is writing.

00:39:26.740 --> 00:39:29.680
You can use this to
write your own queries.

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:32.740
Even better than that, you
can figure out sometimes

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:35.440
how the unnests are done.

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:37.045
Now in this case,
in Data Studio I

00:39:37.045 --> 00:39:40.960
could not filter for two
different properties.

00:39:40.960 --> 00:39:45.180
So I wrote my own query.

00:39:45.180 --> 00:39:47.800
Here I'm looking
for three things.

00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:50.590
I'm looking for an event.

00:39:50.590 --> 00:39:52.410
I only want to look
at the event name

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:54.880
when the round is completed.

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:57.560
And I want to be able
to look at the score

00:39:57.560 --> 00:39:59.660
to filter out if people
are spending money

00:39:59.660 --> 00:40:02.710
or not and how many
powers they have.

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:06.359
And I can run the query here.

00:40:06.359 --> 00:40:07.400
And these are my results.

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:08.970
Like, when the
round is completed,

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:12.120
my first result I
had a score of 4,000.

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:14.860
This person did not
spend any money.

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:17.810
And this person had
this many powers.

00:40:17.810 --> 00:40:21.680
And my next challenge here
is that I want to group this.

00:40:21.680 --> 00:40:22.660
I want to count.

00:40:22.660 --> 00:40:25.640
I want to get my averages.

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:28.540
Grouping by is a
spender is pretty easy.

00:40:28.540 --> 00:40:31.080
I can just group by
is a spender or not.

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:34.670
But how do I
differentiate if I have

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:37.040
people that use a lot
of powers or people

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:40.270
that are not using
a lot of powers?

00:40:40.270 --> 00:40:42.520
First you'll notice that--

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:44.710
as Chad showed you earlier--

00:40:44.710 --> 00:40:46.780
I'm running my unnest.

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:49.840
Not at the from level.

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:52.160
I'm not doing joins
between the unnest.

00:40:52.160 --> 00:40:56.440
I'm just doing these mini
selects at the select level

00:40:56.440 --> 00:40:58.130
where I go through
these properties.

00:40:58.130 --> 00:41:00.850
And I just choose
the ones I want.

00:41:00.850 --> 00:41:07.270
And then with these lines
that I will uncomment here,

00:41:07.270 --> 00:41:09.820
I'm getting the average score.

00:41:09.820 --> 00:41:14.820
I want to know what
score are people getting.

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:16.950
Oh, how did I click that?

00:41:16.950 --> 00:41:18.720
I don't know.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:21.990
So I'm here getting
the average score.

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:25.870
I'm getting the variable if
they are spending or not.

00:41:25.870 --> 00:41:27.670
And I'm differentiating
between people

00:41:27.670 --> 00:41:31.300
that have a lot of powers
or not with an if sentence.

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:34.840
If they have less than 20
powers, it's few powers.

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:37.410
If they have more than
that, it's many powers.

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:43.460
And I will be able to
group by those valuables.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:45.740
So let's see our people here.

00:41:45.740 --> 00:41:46.860
Let me run the query.

00:41:49.660 --> 00:41:50.860
Here I have my results.

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:54.120
This is the average score for
people that are not spenders.

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:57.250
And they are divided
by are they using

00:41:57.250 --> 00:41:59.620
few powers or many powers.

00:41:59.620 --> 00:42:02.780
And these results are
hard to understand.

00:42:02.780 --> 00:42:05.240
Because I have the
numbers here, but

00:42:05.240 --> 00:42:08.650
it would be really nice
if I could visualize them.

00:42:08.650 --> 00:42:11.000
And now in the new
BigQuery UI, we

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:14.860
have this handy button,
Exploring Data Studio.

00:42:14.860 --> 00:42:16.430
And whatever
queries I'm running,

00:42:16.430 --> 00:42:18.530
I can bring back to
Data Studio and just

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:21.500
start visualizing my results.

00:42:21.500 --> 00:42:25.400
So let me do a
scatter chart here.

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:36.100
Let me get on the X side I
will get the average score.

00:42:36.100 --> 00:42:40.040
Let me add in
another column here.

00:42:40.040 --> 00:42:41.540
I just want a count.

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:50.240
This is my count of how many
people I have in every block.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:52.970
Because, yeah, I
have my four groups.

00:42:52.970 --> 00:42:55.460
But I don't have enough data.

00:42:55.460 --> 00:42:59.970
Like, it would be nice
to see how many people we

00:42:59.970 --> 00:43:01.060
have in each group.

00:43:01.060 --> 00:43:04.770
Let me go back to
exploring Data Studio.

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:06.960
Again, I'm getting
my results here.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:10.300
I'm going to do a
scattered chart.

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:15.010
On X, I want to get
the count of people.

00:43:15.010 --> 00:43:18.296
On Y, I want to get
the average score.

00:43:18.296 --> 00:43:27.770
And to make it easy to
differentiate between people

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:33.040
that use a lot of powers or not,
I can add that dimension here.

00:43:33.040 --> 00:43:35.920
So now I have my four
different groups visualized.

00:43:35.920 --> 00:43:39.850
Now I can even style
this to get these people

00:43:39.850 --> 00:43:44.710
in a different color, depending
on if they are spenders or not.

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:47.230
Let's do it like this.

00:43:47.230 --> 00:43:50.172
And now my results make sense.

00:43:50.172 --> 00:43:53.490
Like, who's getting the
highest average score?

00:43:53.490 --> 00:43:55.980
People that have spent money.

00:43:55.980 --> 00:43:57.600
So it's good to spend money.

00:43:57.600 --> 00:43:59.490
And that of many powers.

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:03.030
Even then people have spent
money and don't have money--

00:44:03.030 --> 00:44:04.380
but only have few powers--

00:44:04.380 --> 00:44:07.720
you can visualize here
that get lower scores

00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:10.230
than people that
are not spenders

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:12.520
and have the situation.

00:44:12.520 --> 00:44:15.450
Now, this is a
pretty visualization,

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:18.150
and I want to use it
in my larger dashboard.

00:44:18.150 --> 00:44:20.920
You can just save this.

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:26.150
You can do-- let me
wait until this saves.

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:27.630
It saves.

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:31.450
Now, I can export the report
to an existing report.

00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:36.560
The one we just created
here I will edit it.

00:44:36.560 --> 00:44:40.530
I will create a new
page, because every page

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:44.450
is super full of data already.

00:44:44.450 --> 00:44:51.420
And here I can just Control,
Paste, my new visualization.

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:53.970
And that's how simple it
is to go from Data Studio

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:56.600
to the BigQuery,
create your queries,

00:44:56.600 --> 00:45:00.990
visualize them, and paste
it in your new reports.

00:45:00.990 --> 00:45:03.210
I think it's pretty,
pretty powerful.

00:45:03.210 --> 00:45:06.140
And then the rest of your
team can come here, use it,

00:45:06.140 --> 00:45:08.240
and they don't need
to learn anything

00:45:08.240 --> 00:45:09.860
about how to query BigQuery.

00:45:09.860 --> 00:45:12.240
We just do this for them.

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:17.530
Two important concepts
here is that in this report

00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:20.650
every time someone changes
one variable, filters

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:24.030
by different features, that's
going back to BigQuery.

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:25.190
That is taking time.

00:45:25.190 --> 00:45:27.080
That takes some cost.

00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:29.470
And if you have a lot of
people looking at this,

00:45:29.470 --> 00:45:30.880
your costs could go up.

00:45:30.880 --> 00:45:33.142
There are two things
that you should do.

00:45:33.142 --> 00:45:34.600
One, is when you're
using BigQuery,

00:45:34.600 --> 00:45:36.940
please set up your
cost controls.

00:45:36.940 --> 00:45:39.580
We have nice features
there that will

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:41.590
limit how much you're spending.

00:45:41.590 --> 00:45:44.170
You won't wake up
to any surprise.

00:45:44.170 --> 00:45:45.610
Also there there's
a lot of tricks

00:45:45.610 --> 00:45:49.930
to make the spend use when
you're using Data Studio to go

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:51.310
almost to zero.

00:45:51.310 --> 00:45:54.610
I made a presentation
about that two days ago.

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:55.900
It's already on YouTube.

00:45:55.900 --> 00:45:59.740
Look for it on YouTube or on my
Twitter account, @FelipeHoffa.

